WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
Welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, episode 139.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.500
Today is July 22nd, 2026.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.120
Here's your space and science briefing.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.200
A European spacecraft just cleared its final major test

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.680
before hunting Earth-like worlds around sun-like stars.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.100
The European Space Agency's Play-Doh mission

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.120
has completed its last major spacecraft test

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.800
ahead of a 2027 Ariane 6 launch.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.900
Engineers confirmed the spacecraft's systems

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.560
performed as expected during the final verification campaign.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.220
Once in orbit, the spacecraft will monitor thousands

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.520
of sun-like stars for transiting Earth-sized planets.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.180
The data will help astronomers determine how common,

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.200
potentially habitable worlds are in the Milky Way.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.040
The next milestone is integration with the launch vehicle.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.020
Now, shifting to a different part of the sky,

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.940
astronomers have confirmed a PVATron near the star

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.040
Altair that accelerates protons

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.280
to PETA electron-volt energies.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.860
Gamma ray, radio, and x-ray observations

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.440
from three observatories ruled out alternative explanations.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.120
The source is the first conclusively identified

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.260
galactic accelerator of this class.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.300
A Hiroshima University-led team

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.540
combined the multi-wave length data sets

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:22.780
to reach the conclusion.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.760
The finding opens the search for similar objects elsewhere

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:27.760
in the Milky Way.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.280
On a different note, the IXPE telescope

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.480
has produced the first direct measurement

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.940
of the magnetic field around a pulsar

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.360
in the lighthouse nebula.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.580
The spinning neutron star's extreme environment

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.680
generates structured magnetic fields

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.480
that shape the surrounding nebula.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.880
IXPE's polarization data reveal how the field

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.260
is oriented relative to the pulsar's rotation axis.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.180
This measurement provides a new window

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.900
into the physics of these ultra-dense objects.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.560
Follow-up observations are planned

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.600
to compare the field geometry with theoretical models.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.320
The same extreme objects that generate these fields

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.480
also power missions focused on orbital servicing.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.920
A two-armed robotic spacecraft lifted off

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.700
on a mission to attach life extension jet packs

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.800
to satellites running low on fuel.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.600
The targets orbit thousands of kilometers above Earth.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.920
The servicer will rendezvous with multiple vehicles

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.940
and install new propulsion units.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.920
Success would allow operators to keep existing satellites

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.160
in service longer.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.180
The mission represents the first dedicated

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.080
commercial satellite servicing flight of its kind.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.860
Meanwhile, a Falcon 9 lifted off from Florida

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:47.180
carrying the Northrop Grumman MRV-MEP mission.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.420
All payloads reached orbit and were successfully deployed.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.580
The booster flying this mission has now completed 32 flights

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.160
since its debut in December 2021.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.840
Those flights have delivered cargo to the space station

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.180
and launched dozens of Starlink satellites.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.260
Deployment of every MRV-MEP payload

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.700
was confirmed shortly after separation.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:15.180
Ruseability is also advancing on the other side of the planet.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.880
Orient space completed its third gravity-1 solid rocket flight

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:21.420
from waters near Shanghai.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.640
The 30-meter vehicle placed nine satellites into orbit.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.480
The launch demonstrates continued progress

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.500
for China's commercial launch sector

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.720
using sea-based operations.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.000
All payloads were reported successfully separated

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.120
after liftoff.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.380
The mission marks the latest step

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:42.240
in building a domestic commercial launch capability.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.740
Another nation's commercial sector

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:46.260
is also moving forward.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.420
Skyroot Aerospace's Vikram-1 rocket reached orbit,

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.360
marking the first successful private orbital launch

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:54.040
from India.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.980
The flight demonstrated indigenous solid

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:58.640
and liquid propellant stages.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.760
The company now joins a small group of nations

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.340
with demonstrated private launch capability.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.720
The milestone advances India's growing commercial space sector.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.620
The vehicle lifted off carrying a payload

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.480
that reached its intended orbit.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.580
While one company extends satellite life,

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.060
another booster just added 32 flights to its resume.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:23.860
NASA's psyche mission captured a sequence of images

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:24.940
while passing Mars.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.720
The time lapse shows the planet growing

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.520
and then receding during the gravity assist maneuver.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.120
Mission teams are using the data to refine navigation

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.880
for the asteroid encounter.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.920
The flyby also provided an opportunity

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.620
to test several instruments.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:43.200
The images offer a clear record

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.540
of the spacecraft's trajectory adjustment.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.380
While one spacecraft uses Mars for a speed boost,

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.180
another is preparing technology

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.580
that could one day make fuel on the red planet itself.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.320
The Mars atmospheric reactor for synthesis of consumables

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:01.280
passed its final NASA review.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.820
The Southwest Research Institute

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.980
and University of Texas at San Antonio device

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:09.280
will fly on parabolic aircraft

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.600
to test performance in partial gravity.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.800
The electrolyzer aims to produce oxygen

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.860
and fuel from the Martian atmosphere.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.440
The experiment is now designated ready to fly.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.640
Parabolic flights will simulate

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:24.300
the reduced gravity conditions

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.240
expected on the Martian surface.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:28.700
Global dust storms on Mars

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:30.660
generate strong electric fields

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.580
in addition to blocking sunlight.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:34.560
These fields can interfere

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.380
with spacecraft electronics and solar panels.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.480
Researchers are now modeling

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.760
how the charge builds and discharges

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.300
during planet-wide events.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.100
The work will inform design requirements

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:48.140
for future landers and orbiters.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.680
Only Earth, Mars, and Titan

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.840
are known to host active dust storms

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:54.340
among planetary bodies.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:57.400
From planetary weather to the ice dynamics

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:58.580
of our own world,

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.040
the US, India, Nisar satellites,

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.260
L-band radar captured an image

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.620
of Nunatak, Zatir Jeff's Jizza,

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:08.420
a mountain peak surrounded by flowing ice.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:10.700
Stresses around the obstruction

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.760
create a distinctive fracture pattern

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:14.160
resembling a hummingbird.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.100
Public access to Nisar's L-band

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:19.700
and S-band data began on July 20.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.080
The radar observations help track ice movement

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.740
and surface deformation across Antarctica.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:28.440
Processed files from both instruments

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.760
are being released on an ongoing basis.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:32.940
From Earth's changing ice

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.680
to a brief celestial alignment visible tonight,

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:37.640
Saturn's moon, Dione,

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.600
skims north of the planet's pole

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:43.180
between 3 a.m. and 4 a.m. Eastern daylight time.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:45.140
Observers with telescopes

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:47.260
can watch the 10th magnitude moon

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:48.740
against the ringed planet.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:51.240
The event offers a brief chance

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.280
to see the satellite's position relative

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:54.720
to Saturn's north pole.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.440
The moon appears to travel just above

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.800
the planet's visible limb during the passage.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:02.300
Clear skies and steady seeing

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:04.440
will help observers catch the alignment.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.920
From tonight's sky to a milestone

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:08.860
15 years in the past,

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:13.560
STS 135 touched down 15 years ago today,

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:15.860
closing the space shuttle program.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:19.020
Atlantis completed the 135th

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:20.500
and last mission of the fleet.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:22.940
The landing marked the end of an era

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:24.720
that began in 1981.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.040
The orbiter returned safely

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:28.400
after delivering supplies

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.260
to the International Space Station.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:32.220
No further shuttle flights

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.200
followed the conclusion of the program.

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:36.400
From the end of one program

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.820
to the start of another young person's journey,

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:42.200
Gia Bula from South Africa was selected

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.760
for NASA's United Space School Program.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:48.300
The opportunity gives students hands-on exposure

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.860
to space science and engineering projects.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:53.920
Participants work alongside NASA scientists

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:55.320
during the summer session.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:57.880
The program introduces young people

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:00.020
to mission planning, research methods,

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:01.520
and technical challenges.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.300
Bula's selection highlights international participation

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.640
in NASA educational initiatives.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:09.600
Researchers are examining

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.520
whether the cosmological constant

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.220
remains the best explanation

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:15.720
for the universe's accelerating expansion.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:18.640
The work tests whether current observations

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:20.300
still require a component

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:24.260
that makes up roughly 70% of the universe's energy density.

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:26.940
Alternative models are being compared

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:28.520
against the latest supernova

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:30.480
and large-scale structured data.

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:33.360
The study identifies potential cracks

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.720
in the standard cosmological framework.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:39.320
Ongoing surveys will supply additional constraints

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:41.220
on the nature of the acceleration.

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:44.900
Plenty more cosmic stories are unfolding this week.

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:47.340
Astronomers have now confirmed

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.840
the first p-vitron in the Milky Way

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:52.180
by combining gamma ray, radio,

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.700
and x-ray observations near LT.

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:57.320
The source accelerates protons

00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:59.540
to energies a thousand times higher

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.120
than the most powerful human-made accelerators.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:05.840
This detection finally solves a decades-old puzzle

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:09.120
about where the galaxy's highest energy cosmic rays

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:09.960
originate.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:13.580
The next step is to locate additional p-veitrons

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:15.400
and determine whether they share

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:17.180
the same acceleration mechanism.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.440
If the sun were shrunk to the size of a city,

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.600
its mass would fit inside a neutron star.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.020
A single teaspoon of that material

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:27.820
would weigh as much as Mount Everest.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:30.920
Inside the star, protons and electrons

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:33.060
are crushed together into neutrons

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:36.600
yet a small fraction of charged particles remain free

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:38.800
and race around the ultra-dense core

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:40.240
at near-light speeds.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:43.000
Their motion creates electric currents

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:45.920
that, amplified by the star's rapid rotation,

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:49.740
generate magnetic fields reaching 10 to the 12 ghosts.

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:52.420
These fields are strong enough

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:54.520
to pull the electrons right off atoms.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:56.580
These fields are not uniform.

00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:58.800
They form tangled bundles

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.240
that channel radiation into narrow beams,

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:04.640
producing the lighthouse effect we observe as pulsars.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:08.520
The exact process that maintains such intense fields

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:11.060
over millions of years remains unsolved.

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:14.980
Theorists still cannot explain why some neutron stars

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:18.080
appear to possess even stronger magnetar fields

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:19.440
while others do not.

